AB  - The rapid industrialisation of artificial intelligence has elevated Machine Learning Operations (MLOps) into a foundational discipline for organisations seeking to deploy, monitor, and sustain machine learning models in production. Despite a growing body of literature on technical pipeline orchestration and continuous delivery for ML, significant gaps persist in how enterprises-particularly within the financial services and FinTech sectors-integrate interpretability requirements, address organisational readiness, and govern data-driven decision-making at scale. This paper presents a systematic literature review (SLR) following the PRISMA protocol, synthesising findings from 170 peer-reviewed publications spanning 2015 to 2025. We examine three interconnected themes: (i) MLOps frameworks, maturity models, and pipeline orchestration practices; (ii) explainability and interpretability integration within MLOps lifecycles; and (iii) organisational, team-level, and governance factors that determine successful enterprise MLOps adoption. Drawing on the SPACE and DORA frameworks for developer productivity analogy, we further contextualise these findings within large-scale FinTech settings, highlighting the particular pressures of regulatory compliance (SR 11-7, GDPR, EU AI Act), model risk management, and real-time fraud detection pipelines. We propose a four-tier integrative MLOps Maturity and Interpretability Framework (MMIF) and identify eight priority research gaps requiring empirical investigation. Our findings offer actionable guidance for practitioners, engineering leads, and product managers responsible for operationalising AI within enterprise environments.
