T1  - An empirical study of Policy-as-Code adoption in open-source software projects
AU  - Foalem, Patrick Loic
AU  - Silva, Leuson Da
AU  - Khomh, Foutse
AU  - Merlo, Ettore
JO  - Journal of Systems and Software
VL  - 242
SP  - 113028
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026/12/01/
SN  - 0164-1212
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jss.2026.113028
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S016412122600261X
KW  - Empirical
KW  - Policy as Code
KW  - PaC tools
KW  - Github repository
AB  - Context:
Policy-as-Code (PaC) has become a foundational approach for embedding governance, compliance, and security requirements directly into software systems. While organizations increasingly adopt PaC tools, the software engineering community lacks an empirical understanding of how these tools are used in real-world development practices.
Objective:
This paper aims to bridge this gap by conducting a large-scale study of PaC usage in open-source software. Our goal is to characterize how PaC tools are adopted, what purposes they serve, and what governance activities they support across diverse software ecosystems.
Methods:
We analyzed 399 GitHub repositories using nine widely adopted PaC tools. Our mixed-methods approach combines quantitative analysis of tool usage and project characteristics with a qualitative investigation of policy files. We further employ a Large Language Model (LLM)–assisted classification pipeline, refined through expert validation, to derive a taxonomy of PaC usage consisting of 5 categories and 15 sub-categories.
Results:
Our study reveals substantial diversity in PaC adoption. PaC tools are frequently used in early-stage projects and are heavily oriented toward governance, configuration control, and documentation. We also observe emerging PaC usage in MLOps pipelines and strong co-usage patterns, such as between Gatekeeper and OPA. Our taxonomy highlights recurring governance intents.
Conclusion:
Our findings offer actionable insights for practitioners and tool developers. They highlight concrete usage patterns, emphasize actual PaC usage, and motivate opportunities for improving tool interoperability. This study lays the empirical foundation for future research on PaC practices and their role in ensuring trustworthy, compliant software systems.
ER  - 

